#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084017} :activity/id #uuid "f95be382-38ca-5542-bef3-419ba331df88" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049873 :topic/id #uuid "19f4470c-27bd-57f0-bd24-c70403f1614f" :topic/math-academy-id 725} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084017 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084422 :tutorial/content "A function is said to be an **even function** if it is symmetric about the $y$-axis.\n\nFor example, the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^2\n$$\n\nwhose graph is depicted below, is an example of an even function. It is symmetric about the $y$-axis, meaning that if we reflect the graph over the $y$-axis, we get the same picture.\n\n![](images/cf/cfca345c7ca094cacdbc6d7141a09e34fd116c677629a6a4c1917fb0db1f753f.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "263b7003-1ee6-55aa-94cd-5ee25cbf63a4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2774 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2819e00a-c45a-50f1-a7ea-e3637612c182" :step/math-academy-id 14086 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084049}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084018 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083166 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082983 :question/id #uuid "66135849-b0f8-53a9-aab8-4b6aebde3cd9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3994"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d887d9f-b9c4-54b3-a53e-691989bda962" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048474}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083674}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Odd Functions Graphically"} :step/id #uuid "c9f1a487-6342-5b97-86bc-81ffa4a8d287" :step/math-academy-id 14088 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084019}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084019 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084423 :tutorial/content "Recall that the graph of the even function\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^2\n$$\n\nis symmetric about the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/07/07d0699c46b3cbd215c461d5df3629d86cd31a93228d6790fdc65b383e8556fc.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-1) &= 1 = f(1) \\\\\nf(-2) &= 4 = f(2) \\\\\nf(-3) &= 9 = f(3) \\\\\n&=⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for every $x$ in the domain, both $x$ and $-x$ give the same value of the function. Algebraically, this fact can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nf(-x) = f(x)\n$$\n\nIn general, a function $f(x)$ is said to be **even** if\n\n$$\nf(-x) = f(x)\n$$\n\nfor each $x$ in the domain of the function." :tutorial/id #uuid "4c7a0bb9-4765-505f-a538-dd4a1426f8c3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2776 :tutorial/title "The Algebraic Definition of Even Functions"} :step/id #uuid "95f32820-f0d3-5c90-856a-b03f017bb399" :step/math-academy-id 14090 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084051}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084020 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083167 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082984 :question/id #uuid "5b22df97-b731-5d77-a6ed-2a90efcd95a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-3996"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bff4063-9e51-53fa-ab55-e68540e6f587" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083675}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Expression Given the Table for an Even or Odd Function"} :step/id #uuid "6f3a0616-90b9-5a24-8eb2-82152eed463a" :step/math-academy-id 14091} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084049 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083184 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082992 :question/id #uuid "f8d678e6-c0db-534d-9ae2-0551ea8201bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-4172"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb0d0f84-a628-5605-a75f-f4f5459e047c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050151}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083673}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Even Functions Graphically"} :step/id #uuid "8c6a848e-062e-550a-98cc-a5b33fc8efb8" :step/math-academy-id 15131 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084050}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084050 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084430 :tutorial/content "A function is said to be an **odd function** if it has **rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin**. That is to say, if we rotate the graph of an odd function by $180^\\circ$ about the origin, we end up with the same picture.\n\nFor example, the function\n$g(x)=x$,\nwhose graph is shown below, is an example of an odd function. It has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, meaning that if we rotate the graph of an odd function by $180^\\circ$ about the origin, we end up with the same picture.\n\n![](images/ff/ff49c826d9a6a36b35edf73689d30e5d9dadc976b5f1caf47c1dd0383f9893bc.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "1184d837-2701-5c4d-806c-6af72dee583b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2840 :tutorial/title "Classifying Odd Functions Graphically"} :step/id #uuid "e25c1d87-8e4c-58bc-98db-694f2852ceb3" :step/math-academy-id 15132 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084018}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084051 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084431 :tutorial/content "Now, recall that the graph of the odd function $g(x)=x$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin.\n\n![](images/a0/a0dd6a5cbc09182a4a87569879a97da697a21eeda869ae20f512dc86a911b75e.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-1) &= -1 =-f(1) \\\\\nf(-2) &= -2 =-f(2) \\\\\nf(-3) &= -3 =-f(3) \\\\\n&=⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for every $x$ in the domain, $x$ and $-x$ give opposite values of the function. Algebraically, this fact can be written as\n\n$$\nf(-x) = -f(x)\n$$\n\nIn general, a function $f(x)$ is said to be **odd** if\n\n$$\nf(-x) = -f(x)\n$$\n\nfor each $x$ in the domain of the function." :tutorial/id #uuid "db19e6ba-49d7-534c-b84b-24dfd8866658" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2844 :tutorial/title "The Algebraic Definition of Odd Functions"} :step/id #uuid "46e959d5-c850-58b6-aabc-e6143b9e3a64" :step/math-academy-id 15142 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084052}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084052 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083185 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082993 :question/id #uuid "373bdf54-aaa4-5e94-82a7-95c0a396623a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4183"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fa847d9-f0bf-57fb-92b2-27c8a7cdb3b1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049317}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234216}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Even and Odd Functions Using the Algebraic Definition"} :step/id #uuid "b60bbea3-5236-5bc1-93fe-54234cf0b4fb" :step/math-academy-id 15151 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084020}}] :activity/title "Even and Odd Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083420}]}
